    {
      "type": "heading",
      "continuation": false,
      "heading_level": 2,
      "md_text": "DUAL-PLAYFIELD PRIORITY AND CONTROL",
      "bbox": [
        357,
        1701,
        1353,
        1746
      ],
      "segment_id": "page_0112_seg_005"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "continuation": false,
      "heading_level": null,
      "md_text": "Either playfield 1 or 2 may have priority; that is, either one may be displayed in front of the other. Playfield 1 normally has priority. The bit known as PF2PRI (bit 6) in register BPLCON2 is used to control priority. When PF2PRI = 1, playfield 2 has priority over playfield 1. When PF2PRI = 0, playfield 1 has priority.",
      "bbox": [
        356,
        1840,
        2156,
        2045
      ],
      "segment_id": "page_0112_seg_006"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "continuation": false,
      "heading_level": null,
      "md_text": "You can also control the relative priority of playfields and sprites. Chapter 7, “System Control Hardware,” shows you how to control the priority of these objects.",
      "bbox": [
        356,
        2108,
        2156,
        2206
      ],
      "segment_id": "page_0112_seg_007"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "continuation": false,
      "heading_level": null,
      "md_text": "You can control the two playfields separately as follows:",
      "bbox": [
        356,
        2271,
        1388,
        2315
      ],
      "segment_id": "page_0112_seg_008"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "continuation": false,
      "heading_level": null,
      "md_text": "- They can have different-sized representations in memory, and different portions of each one can be selected for display.\n- They can be scrolled separately.",
      "bbox": [
        440,
        2378,
        2158,
        2580
      ],
      "segment_id": "page_0112_seg_009"
    },
